# [Project name]

- **Follow** [path to PROJECT_BRIEF, e.g. agentic_hangout/PROJECT_BRIEF.md] for project context, pipeline, and conventions.
- **New scripts** and **AI-related docs** go in **`agentic_hangout/`**. **Outputs** from those scripts (CSVs, figures, tables) also go in **`agentic_hangout/`** (or e.g. `agentic_hangout/output/`)—never in main data/output folders unless the user explicitly approves.
- **Approved edits** to the main pipeline go in **[code/ or scripts/ — replace with your folder]**.
- **Prefer R** and existing packages; run from **project root** so relative paths work.

- **Protected folders:** Do not create, modify, or delete files in **`code/`**, **`data_raw/`**, **`data_processed/`**, **`output/`**, or **`.cursor/rules/`** unless the user explicitly asks you to. Propose changes in agentic_hangout first; only touch these folders when the user approves.

- Style: "Use tidyverse-style R (dplyr, tidyr, etc.) where it fits. Prefer readable pipes and explicit column names; avoid obscure base-R tricks."
- Reproducibility: "Assume the working directory is the project root. Use relative paths from project root or here::here(); do not hardcode absolute paths."
- Approved scripts: "Do not edit scripts in the main folder unless the user explicitly asks. Propose new logic in scripts under agentic_hangout/ first."
- New/experimental: "New R scripts and AI-related docs go in agentic_hangout/. Only move or copy into the main script folder when the user approves."
- Outputs: "Figures and tables from agentic_hangout scripts go in agentic_hangout/ or agentic_hangout/output/; do not overwrite main output folders without being asked."
- Naming: "Name new scripts clearly and keep a short comment at the top describing what the script does."
- Explain before big changes: "For non-trivial changes, briefly describe the plan before editing. For small edits, just do it."
- One concern per script when possible: "New scripts in agentic_hangout/ should focus on one concern so they are easy to review and optionally merge."
- In-code comments: "Add short comments for non-obvious steps. Keep comments in sync with the code."
